  "body": "(not sure if this is the best place to put this)\n\nRegarding `URLSearchParams` constructor string param:\nIMO it would be good to skip leading `?` if present, before starting to do the real parsing.\n\nThe reason is that `window.location.search` returns `?a=1&b=2` with leading `?`.\nFor consistency the URLSearchParams constructor should allow the leading `?` thus.\n\nLooking at the spec and test they don't explicitly mention anything about leading `?`\n\nhttps://url.spec.whatwg.org/#interface-urlsearchparams\nhttps://github.com/w3c/web-platform-tests/blob/0ae07c6dec055b416e91a8e0e431eec8c2b2552b/url/urlsearchparams-get.html (master as of today)\n\nIn the current Firefox stable 44 and Chrome Canary 50 implementations this happens:\n\n`new URLSearchParams('a=1&b=2').get('a') == 1`\n`new URLSearchParams('?a=1&b=2').get('a') == null`\n`new URLSearchParams('?a=1&b=2').get('?a') == 1`\n\nwhat if instead we skipped leading `?` if present and return this:\n\n`new URLSearchParams('?a=1&b=2').get('a') == 1`\n`new URLSearchParams('?a=1&b=2').get('?a') == null`\n\nWhat do you think?\n",
